    {
      "filename": "Basalt Rock Formation - geograph.org.uk - 180882.jpg",
      "title": "File:Basalt Rock Formation - geograph.org.uk - 180882.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Basalt_Rock_Formation_-_geograph.org.uk_-_180882.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basalt_Rock_Formation_-_geograph.org.uk_-_180882.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 480,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Mick Garratt",
      "description": "Basalt Rock Formation, in the Mull Lava Group of Palaeocene age. Taken on the way to the fossilised tree. I am indebted to Keith Burns for this explanation of the fascinating geological processes involved here :\n\"On the beach, basalt columns radiate from a central point on the\nshore near the fossil tree.  The columns are formed due to the cooling\neffect of a standing tree on the lava flowing around the tree trunk.\nThe tree draws heat from the lava. When the lava shrinks, it forms\nhexagonal shrinkage cracks which form columns whose axes are parallel to the\ndirection of heat flow.  So the hexagonal columns radiate from the tree\ntrunk which is drawing heat from the lava.\nIf lava is in a level slab losing heat to the air above it, it cools from\nthe top down and forms the more familiar vertical hexagonal columns (e.g.\nGiant's Causeway, Staffa, Sampson's Ribs on Arthur's Seat in Edinburgh).\n\nThere must have been a forest that was engulfed by the slowly flowing lava.\"",
      "dateCreated": "2006-05-28",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "isle-of-mull-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gf59",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/f/5/9/isle-of-mull-wp/basalt-rock-formation-geograph-small.webp"
    },
